<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moca, Dominican Republic</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Moca is a city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cibao">Cibao</a> valley of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>, capital of Espaillat Province. In 1805 Haitian forces retreating from their siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines">Jean-Jacques Dessalines</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-christophe">Henri Christophe</a> sacked the town and killed many of its inhabitants. In 1899 the dictator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulises-heureaux">Ulises Heureaux</a> was assassinated there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moca-dominican-republic.jpg" alt="Moca, Dominican Republic" width="320" height="467" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mercedes Ramirez Guerrero · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iglesia_Sagrado_Corazon_de_Jesus_Moca.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Moca is the capital of Espaillat province in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cibao">Cibao</a> region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>, 18 kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>, and has a population of 164,022 inhabitants. It covers 339.21 square kilometres and sits on a volcanic coral reef, with hills to the west and low mountains to the north that form part of the Cordillera Septentrional. The highest mountain in the municipality and the province is El Mogote, at 971 metres, and the city itself lies at an elevation of 183 metres. It is divided into eight municipal districts: San Víctor, Las Lagunas, José Contreras, Juan López, El Higuerito, La Ortega, Monte de la Jagua and Canca La Reina.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When the Spanish colonised the island, the land of what is now Moca belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdom</a> of Maguá. Moca itself was founded in 1780. In 1845 it was designated a common of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of La Vega, and in the later political division it became the head municipality of Espaillat province.</p><p>The city is known as La Villa Heroica, the Village of Heroes, because of the number of men and women from Moca who played a major part in the nation's history in bringing down two dictators, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulises-heureaux">Ulises Heureaux</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-trujillo">Rafael Trujillo</a>, and in bringing democracy back to the country. Among the people of Moca is Trina de Moya, a poet who was First Lady of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a> in 1899, 1902 to 1903 and 1924 to 1930.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Agriculture is the main livelihood of the inhabitants, with plantain and yucca the chief crops, and most crops are harvested by hand. Other produce includes bananas, pigeon pea, taro, yams, squash, beans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, citrus fruit and papaya, and the city has a large poultry sector. Moca has an important banking sector, with branches of all the country's commercial banks, and a strong trade in shops, furniture, supermarkets and warehouses, along with factories making footwear, food and hardware. It also has the country's largest coffee manufacturing industry, Industries Banilejas.</p><p>The Cathedral of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-heart">Sacred Heart of Jesus</a> stands in the city; its glass windows were brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and show the apostles and the path of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> to the crucifixion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Moca, Dominican Republic” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He repulsed Jean-Jacques Dessalines, whose army besieged Santo Domingo in 1805 and devastated Santiago and <b>Moca</b> on its retreat.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-santo-domingo">French Santo Domingo</a></li></ul></section>
